{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-acc6af62a6ff32f0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttags:\n3\t  - guide\n4\t  - git\n5\t  - documentation\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\tGuide for writing consistent, useful CHANGELOG.md entries in dark-factory projects.\n9\t\n10\t## Goal\n11\t\n12\tProduce changelog entries with conventional prefixes so dark-factory can determine the version bump automatically — no guessing from prose.\n13\t\n14\t## File Structure\n15\t\n16\t```markdown\n17\t# Changelog\n18\t\n19\tAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n20\t\n21\tPlease choose versions by [Semantic Versioning](http://semver.org/).\n22\t\n23\t* MAJOR version when you make incompatible API changes,\n24\t* MINOR version when you add functionality in a backwards-compatible manner, and\n25\t* PATCH version when you make backwards-compatible bug fixes.\n26\t\n27\t## Unreleased\n28\t\n29\t- feat: Add SpecWatcher to monitor specs/ for approved status changes\n30\t- fix: Remove stale Docker container before starting a new executor run\n31\t\n32\t## v1.5.0\n33\t\n34\t- feat: Add FuncRunner interface for executing functions with custom behavior\n35\t- fix: Fix WaiterUntil to handle equal times correctly\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\t### RULE changelog/preamble-frozen (MUST)\n39\t\n40\t**Owner**: agent-auditor\n41\t**Applies when**: a CHANGELOG.md edit inserts content above the `# Changelog` title, modifies the SemVer preamble bullets (MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH), or places a `## Unreleased` / `## vX.Y.Z` section inside (rather than after) the preamble block.\n42\t**Enforcement**: `scripts/rule-checks.sh` (checks `# Changelog` is first content; flags any `## Unreleased`/`## vX.Y.Z` before the preamble block)\n43\t**Why**: The preamble is the API contract between the changelog and every tool that parses it (dark-factory's version-bump detector, /coding:commit's CHANGELOG validator, downstream release-notes generators). Moving / deleting / shifting it breaks the parsers silently — the next release attempt either bumps the wrong version or misses entries entirely. Restoring is cheap; preventing the edit is cheaper.\n44\t\n45\t#### Bad\n46\t\n47\t```markdown\n48\t## Unreleased\n49\t- feat: new thing\n50\t\n51\t# Changelog                          ← preamble shoved below\n52\tAll notable changes...\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\t#### Good\n56\t\n57\t```markdown\n58\t# Changelog\n59\tAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n60\tPlease choose versions by [Semantic Versioning](http://semver.org/).\n61\t* MAJOR / MINOR / PATCH bullets here\n62\t\n63\t## Unreleased\n64\t- feat: new thing\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t**Rules:**\n68\t- Preamble with SemVer explanation always present\n69\t- **Header is frozen**: everything from the start of the file to the FIRST `##` heading (the `# Changelog` title, the \"All notable changes...\" line, the SemVer link, and the MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH bullets) MUST NOT be moved, deleted, or have anything inserted above or inside it. Insert `## Unreleased` (or any version section) immediately AFTER the last header line — never before any header line. If the header is incomplete, restore it; never leave it partial.\n70\t- `## Unreleased` on feature branches — never a version number\n71\t- `## vX.Y.Z` on master — no date suffix\n72\t- Newest version first — `## Unreleased` goes directly above the highest `## vX.Y.Z`\n73\t- Flat list — no `### Added` / `### Fixed` categories\n74\t\n75\t## Conventional Prefixes (REQUIRED)\n76\t\n77\t### RULE changelog/conventional-prefix-required (MUST)\n78\t\n79\t**Owner**: agent-auditor\n80\t**Applies when**: a bullet under `## Unreleased` in CHANGELOG.md does not start with one of the recognised conventional prefixes (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, `perf:`).\n81\t**Enforcement**: judgment (regex over `## Unreleased` bullets: `^- ([a-z]+:)` first token must be in the allowed prefix set)\n82\t**Trigger**: CHANGELOG.md\n83\t**Why**: dark-factory and `/coding:commit` parse the prefix to decide the version bump automatically — any `feat:` entry triggers a minor bump, everything else triggers a patch. Missing or wrong prefix means the version-bump detection fails: the release may patch-bump a feature commit (downstream consumers miss the new functionality in their range queries) or minor-bump a chore. Standardising the prefix is the cheapest possible structure for unambiguous machine parsing.\n84\t\n85\t#### Bad\n86\t\n87\t```markdown\n88\t## Unreleased\n89\t- Add SpecWatcher                    ← no prefix\n90\t- update go and deps                 ← no prefix\n91\t- fix and refactor                   ← ambiguous; multiple prefixes\n92\t```\n93\t\n94\t#### Good\n95\t\n96\t```markdown\n97\t## Unreleased\n98\t- feat: Add SpecWatcher to monitor specs/ for approved status changes\n99\t- chore: Update Go from 1.25.5 to 1.26.0\n100\t- refactor: Extract worktree cleanup to reduce cognitive complexity\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tEvery `## Unreleased` entry must start with a conventional prefix:\n104\t\n105\t| Prefix | Meaning | Version bump |\n106\t|--------|---------|-------------|\n107\t| `feat:` | New feature or capability | **Minor** (`vX.Y+1.0`) |\n108\t| `fix:` | Bug fix | Patch |\n109\t| `refactor:` | Code restructure, no behavior change | Patch |\n110\t| `test:` | Test additions or improvements | Patch |\n111\t| `docs:` | Documentation only | Patch |\n112\t| `chore:` | Dependency updates, build, tooling | Patch |\n113\t| `perf:` | Performance improvement | Patch |\n114\t\n115\tdark-factory reads these prefixes to determine the version bump automatically. Any `feat:` entry → minor bump; everything else → patch bump.\n116\t\n117\t## Every Source-Changing PR Needs an Unreleased Entry (REQUIRED)\n118\t\n119\t### RULE changelog/unreleased-entry-required (SHOULD)\n120\t\n121\t**Owner**: agent-auditor\n122\t**Applies when**: the repo root contains a `CHANGELOG.md`, the PR changes at least one non-vendored file, and the PR-HEAD `CHANGELOG.md` has no `-` bullet under its `## Unreleased` heading (the section is absent or empty).\n123\t**Enforcement**: script (`scripts/rule-checks.sh`) — diff-scoped only; when ≥1 non-vendored file changed and `CHANGELOG.md` exists at the repo root, scan the `## Unreleased` section of the PR-HEAD `CHANGELOG.md` for a `-`/`*` bullet and flag when none is present. Deterministic state-check — does not depend on the review model executing a judgment step (so it fires reliably even on a weak review model). Trade-offs: no trivial-diff waiver (a comment/whitespace-only change still needs a bullet), and a `## Unreleased` already populated by an earlier unreleased merge suppresses the flag.\n124\t**Why**: In an `autoRelease: true` repo the release agent renames `## Unreleased` → `## vX.Y.Z` after merge. A PR merged with an empty `## Unreleased` makes the release agent no-op — the change reaches `master` but never ships to consumers. Requiring one bullet per source-changing PR is the forcing function that keeps every merge releasable. Gate on `CHANGELOG.md` presence alone, not `autoRelease` — manual-release repos also need the section current for the human releaser.\n125\t\n126\t#### Bad\n127\t\n128\t```markdown\n129\t# PR edits pkg/foo/foo.go and adds no CHANGELOG.md entry.\n130\t# ## Unreleased stays empty → after merge the release agent has\n131\t# nothing to promote → no version ships.\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\t#### Good\n135\t\n136\t```markdown\n137\t## Unreleased\n138\t\n139\t- feat: Add Foo.Bar to expose the widget count on the status endpoint\n140\t```\n141\t\n142\t**Rules:**\n143\t- Every PR that changes a non-vendored file in a repo with a `CHANGELOG.md` adds at least one `## Unreleased` bullet (with a conventional prefix per the rule above).\n144\t- Deterministic state-check — the check reads the PR-HEAD `## Unreleased` section, not the diff, so it needs no base-branch resolution and fires regardless of the review model. It intentionally has **no** trivial-diff waiver.\n145\t- Not a version-string bump — the entry is a bullet under `## Unreleased`, never a manifest edit (see § Version Alignment Is Release-Time).\n146\t\n147\t## Entry Style\n148\t\n149\t**Format:** `- <prefix>: <what> [context]`\n150\t\n151\t**Be specific:**\n152\t- Name the exact type, function, command, or package touched\n153\t- Include versions for dependency updates\n154\t- Add brief context for non-obvious changes\n155\t\n156\t## Anti-Patterns\n157\t\n158\t❌ `- Add SpecWatcher` — missing prefix, bump detection fails\n159\t✅ `- feat: Add SpecWatcher to monitor specs/ for approved status changes`\n160\t\n161\t❌ `- feat: update go and deps` — wrong prefix (chore), and too vague\n162\t✅ `- chore: Update Go from 1.25.5 to 1.26.0`\n163\t✅ `- chore: Update github.com/bborbe/errors to v1.5.2`\n164\t\n165\t❌ `- fix: refactor` — what was refactored?\n166\t✅ `- refactor: Extract worktree cleanup to reduce cognitive complexity`\n167\t\n168\t❌ `- test: add tests` — for what?\n169\t✅ `- test: Add processor test suite covering retry and failure paths (12 tests)`\n170\t\n171\t❌ `- fix: fix bug` — which bug?\n172\t✅ `- fix: Fix NormalizeFilenames number conflict on non-standard filename format`\n173\t\n174\t## Merge Conflicts in Unreleased\n175\t\n176\tMultiple feature branches writing to `## Unreleased` will conflict. Resolution: keep both bullet lists, remove conflict markers.\n177\t\n178\t```markdown\n179\t## Unreleased\n180\t- feat: Add SpecWatcher\n181\t- fix: Remove stale container before run\n182\t```\n183\t\n184\t## Version Alignment Is Release-Time, Not Feature-PR-Time\n185\t\n186\tIn repos with `.maintainer.yaml: release.autoRelease: true` (the bborbe default — coding, vault-cli, dark-factory, semantic-search, …), a feature PR:\n187\t\n188\t- adds bullets under `## Unreleased`\n189\t- does **NOT** bump version strings in `plugin.json` / `marketplace.json` / `pyproject.toml` / `Cargo.toml`\n190\t\n191\tDuring development all version strings stay at the **last released** version and remain mutually equal — that is the correct, aligned state. The release agent renames `## Unreleased` → `## vX.Y.Z` and bumps every manifest together **post-merge**; the four-string alignment is a release-time gate (`make release-check`), not a feature-PR gate.\n192\t\n193\t**Review guidance — do NOT flag as a violation:**\n194\t\n195\t- an `## Unreleased` section above the latest `## vX.Y.Z`\n196\t- manifest versions equal to the latest *released* `## vX.Y.Z` while `## Unreleased` holds pending bullets — they are aligned; `## Unreleased` is not a version string\n197\t\n198\tOnly flag misalignment when the top **versioned** `## vX.Y.Z` entry disagrees with a manifest. `## Unreleased` never counts as a version.\n199\t\n200\t## Validation\n201\t\n202\t- [ ] Every `## Unreleased` entry has a conventional prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`, etc.)\n203\t- [ ] `feat:` used only for genuine new features/capabilities\n204\t- [ ] Descriptions are specific — name types, functions, commands\n205\t- [ ] Dependency updates use `chore:` and include version numbers\n206\t- [ ] No vague entries (`fix: fix bug`, `chore: update deps`)\n207\t- [ ] Preamble present with SemVer link\n208\t- [ ] Newest version at top\n209\t- [ ] In an autoRelease repo, the feature PR did NOT bump manifest version strings — `## Unreleased` above the last released `## vX.Y.Z` with manifests still at that released version is correct, not a violation\n210\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79165e9f-87b0-4e96-8a77-dd19caa6f4f6","uuid":"99018155-80ea-4c61-aa17-b0c6cee86303","timestamp":"2026-09-03T19:17:13.067Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/changelog-guide.md","content":"---\ntags:\n  - guide\n  - git\n  - documentation\n---\n\nGuide for writing consistent, useful CHANGELOG.md entries in dark-factory projects.\n\n## Goal\n\nProduce changelog entries with conventional prefixes so dark-factory can determine the version bump automatically — no guessing from prose.\n\n## File Structure\n\n```markdown\n# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n\nPlease choose versions by [Semantic Versioning](http://semver.org/).\n\n* MAJOR version when you make incompatible API changes,\n* MINOR version when you add functionality in a backwards-compatible manner, and\n* PATCH version when you make backwards-compatible bug fixes.\n\n## Unreleased\n\n- feat: Add SpecWatcher to monitor specs/ for approved status changes\n- fix: Remove stale Docker container before starting a new executor run\n\n## v1.5.0\n\n- feat: Add FuncRunner interface for executing functions with custom behavior\n- fix: Fix WaiterUntil to handle equal times correctly\n```\n\n### RULE changelog/preamble-frozen (MUST)\n\n**Owner**: agent-auditor\n**Applies when**: a CHANGELOG.md edit inserts content above the `# Changelog` title, modifies the SemVer preamble bullets (MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH), or places a `## Unreleased` / `## vX.Y.Z` section inside (rather than after) the preamble block.\n**Enforcement**: `scripts/rule-checks.sh` (checks `# Changelog` is first content; flags any `## Unreleased`/`## vX.Y.Z` before the preamble block)\n**Why**: The preamble is the API contract between the changelog and every tool that parses it (dark-factory's version-bump detector, /coding:commit's CHANGELOG validator, downstream release-notes generators). Moving / deleting / shifting it breaks the parsers silently — the next release attempt either bumps the wrong version or misses entries entirely. Restoring is cheap; preventing the edit is cheaper.\n\n#### Bad\n\n```markdown\n## Unreleased\n- feat: new thing\n\n# Changelog                          ← preamble shoved below\nAll notable changes...\n```\n\n#### Good\n\n```markdown\n# Changelog\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\nPlease choose versions by [Semantic Versioning](http://semver.org/).\n* MAJOR / MINOR / PATCH bullets here\n\n## Unreleased\n- feat: new thing\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- Preamble with SemVer explanation always present\n- **Header is frozen**: everything from the start of the file to the FIRST `##` heading (the `# Changelog` title, the \"All notable changes...\" line, the SemVer link, and the MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH bullets) MUST NOT be moved, deleted, or have anything inserted above or inside it. Insert `## Unreleased` (or any version section) immediately AFTER the last header line — never before any header line. If the header is incomplete, restore it; never leave it partial.\n- `## Unreleased` on feature branches — never a version number\n- `## vX.Y.Z` on master — no date suffix\n- Newest version first — `## Unreleased` goes directly above the highest `## vX.Y.Z`\n- Flat list — no `### Added` / `### Fixed` categories\n\n## Conventional Prefixes (REQUIRED)\n\n### RULE changelog/conventional-prefix-required (MUST)\n\n**Owner**: agent-auditor\n**Applies when**: a bullet under `## Unreleased` in CHANGELOG.md does not start with one of the recognised conventional prefixes (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, `perf:`).\n**Enforcement**: judgment (regex over `## Unreleased` bullets: `^- ([a-z]+:)` first token must be in the allowed prefix set)\n**Trigger**: CHANGELOG.md\n**Why**: dark-factory and `/coding:commit` parse the prefix to decide the version bump automatically — any `feat:` entry triggers a minor bump, everything else triggers a patch. Missing or wrong prefix means the version-bump detection fails: the release may patch-bump a feature commit (downstream consumers miss the new functionality in their range queries) or minor-bump a chore. Standardising the prefix is the cheapest possible structure for unambiguous machine parsing.\n\n#### Bad\n\n```markdown\n## Unreleased\n- Add SpecWatcher                    ← no prefix\n- update go and deps                 ← no prefix\n- fix and refactor                   ← ambiguous; multiple prefixes\n```\n\n#### Good\n\n```markdown\n## Unreleased\n- feat: Add SpecWatcher to monitor specs/ for approved status changes\n- chore: Update Go from 1.25.5 to 1.26.0\n- refactor: Extract worktree cleanup to reduce cognitive complexity\n```\n\nEvery `## Unreleased` entry must start with a conventional prefix:\n\n| Prefix | Meaning | Version bump |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `feat:` | New feature or capability | **Minor** (`vX.Y+1.0`) |\n| `fix:` | Bug fix | Patch |\n| `refactor:` | Code restructure, no behavior change | Patch |\n| `test:` | Test additions or improvements | Patch |\n| `docs:` | Documentation only | Patch |\n| `chore:` | Dependency updates, build, tooling | Patch |\n| `perf:` | Performance improvement | Patch |\n\ndark-factory reads these prefixes to determine the version bump automatically. Any `feat:` entry → minor bump; everything else → patch bump.\n\n## Every Source-Changing PR Needs an Unreleased Entry (REQUIRED)\n\n### RULE changelog/unreleased-entry-required (SHOULD)\n\n**Owner**: agent-auditor\n**Applies when**: the repo root contains a `CHANGELOG.md`, the PR changes at least one non-vendored file, and the PR-HEAD `CHANGELOG.md` has no `-` bullet under its `## Unreleased` heading (the section is absent or empty).\n**Enforcement**: script (`scripts/rule-checks.sh`) — diff-scoped only; when ≥1 non-vendored file changed and `CHANGELOG.md` exists at the repo root, scan the `## Unreleased` section of the PR-HEAD `CHANGELOG.md` for a `-`/`*` bullet and flag when none is present. Deterministic state-check — does not depend on the review model executing a judgment step (so it fires reliably even on a weak review model). Trade-offs: no trivial-diff waiver (a comment/whitespace-only change still needs a bullet), and a `## Unreleased` already populated by an earlier unreleased merge suppresses the flag.\n**Why**: In an `autoRelease: true` repo the release agent renames `## Unreleased` → `## vX.Y.Z` after merge. A PR merged with an empty `## Unreleased` makes the release agent no-op — the change reaches `master` but never ships to consumers. Requiring one bullet per source-changing PR is the forcing function that keeps every merge releasable. Gate on `CHANGELOG.md` presence alone, not `autoRelease` — manual-release repos also need the section current for the human releaser.\n\n#### Bad\n\n```markdown\n# PR edits pkg/foo/foo.go and adds no CHANGELOG.md entry.\n# ## Unreleased stays empty → after merge the release agent has\n# nothing to promote → no version ships.\n```\n\n#### Good\n\n```markdown\n## Unreleased\n\n- feat: Add Foo.Bar to expose the widget count on the status endpoint\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- Every PR that changes a non-vendored file in a repo with a `CHANGELOG.md` adds at least one `## Unreleased` bullet (with a conventional prefix per the rule above).\n- Deterministic state-check — the check reads the PR-HEAD `## Unreleased` section, not the diff, so it needs no base-branch resolution and fires regardless of the review model. It intentionally has **no** trivial-diff waiver.\n- Not a version-string bump — the entry is a bullet under `## Unreleased`, never a manifest edit (see § Version Alignment Is Release-Time).\n\n## Entry Style\n\n**Format:** `- <prefix>: <what> [context]`\n\n**Be specific:**\n- Name the exact type, function, command, or package touched\n- Include versions for dependency updates\n- Add brief context for non-obvious changes\n\n## Anti-Patterns\n\n❌ `- Add SpecWatcher` — missing prefix, bump detection fails\n✅ `- feat: Add SpecWatcher to monitor specs/ for approved status changes`\n\n❌ `- feat: update go and deps` — wrong prefix (chore), and too vague\n✅ `- chore: Update Go from 1.25.5 to 1.26.0`\n✅ `- chore: Update github.com/bborbe/errors to v1.5.2`\n\n❌ `- fix: refactor` — what was refactored?\n✅ `- refactor: Extract worktree cleanup to reduce cognitive complexity`\n\n❌ `- test: add tests` — for what?\n✅ `- test: Add processor test suite covering retry and failure paths (12 tests)`\n\n❌ `- fix: fix bug` — which bug?\n✅ `- fix: Fix NormalizeFilenames number conflict on non-standard filename format`\n\n## Merge Conflicts in Unreleased\n\nMultiple feature branches writing to `## Unreleased` will conflict. Resolution: keep both bullet lists, remove conflict markers.\n\n```markdown\n## Unreleased\n- feat: Add SpecWatcher\n- fix: Remove stale container before run\n```\n\n## Version Alignment Is Release-Time, Not Feature-PR-Time\n\nIn repos with `.maintainer.yaml: release.autoRelease: true` (the bborbe default — coding, vault-cli, dark-factory, semantic-search, …), a feature PR:\n\n- adds bullets under `## Unreleased`\n- does **NOT** bump version strings in `plugin.json` / `marketplace.json` / `pyproject.toml` / `Cargo.toml`\n\nDuring development all version strings stay at the **last released** version and remain mutually equal — that is the correct, aligned state. The release agent renames `## Unreleased` → `## vX.Y.Z` and bumps every manifest together **post-merge**; the four-string alignment is a release-time gate (`make release-check`), not a feature-PR gate.\n\n**Review guidance — do NOT flag as a violation:**\n\n- an `## Unreleased` section above the latest `## vX.Y.Z`\n- manifest versions equal to the latest *released* `## vX.Y.Z` while `## Unreleased` holds pending bullets — they are aligned; `## Unreleased` is not a version string\n\nOnly flag misalignment when the top **versioned** `## vX.Y.Z` entry disagrees with a manifest. `## Unreleased` never counts as a version.\n\n## Validation\n\n- [ ] Every `## Unreleased` entry has a conventional prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`, etc.)\n- [ ] `feat:` used only for genuine new features/capabilities\n- [ ] Descriptions are specific — name types, functions, commands\n- [ ] Dependency updates use `chore:` and include version numbers\n- [ ] No vague entries (`fix: fix bug`, `chore: update deps`)\n- [ ] Preamble present with SemVer link\n- [ ] Newest version at top\n- [ ] In an autoRelease repo, the feature PR did NOT bump manifest version strings — `## Unreleased` above the last released `## vX.Y.Z` with manifests still at that released version is correct, not a violation\n","numLines":210,"startLine":1,"totalLines":210}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8db4c1463aa520c2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Documentation Guide\n2\t\n3\t## 1. Overview of Documentation Structure\n4\t\n5\tEach project uses three layers:\n6\t\n7\t    README.md        → What it does, install, use (for humans on GitHub)\n8\t    CLAUDE.md        → How to change the code safely (for AI agents)\n9\t    docs/            → Detailed and in-depth documentation (for both)\n10\t    docs/adr/        → (Optional) Architecture Decision Records\n11\t    docs/prd/        → Product Requirement Definitions\n12\t\n13\t### What Goes Where\n14\t\n15\t| Content | README.md | CLAUDE.md | docs/ |\n16\t|---------|-----------|-----------|-------|\n17\t| What the project does | Yes | 1 line only | Overview |\n18\t| Install/usage | Yes | Never | - |\n19\t| Features/marketing | Yes | Never | - |\n20\t| Build/test commands | Brief | Detailed | - |\n21\t| Architecture | Never | Package map | Full detail |\n22\t| Design decisions | Never | Constraints list | ADRs |\n23\t| Env vars/config | User-facing | Agent-needs | Full reference |\n24\t| API docs | Brief | Never | Full detail |\n25\t| Workflow rules | Never | Yes | - |\n26\t\n27\t### Detailed Guides\n28\t\n29\t- **[readme-guide.md](readme-guide.md)** — How to write a good README.md (templates, sections, project types)\n30\t- **[claude-md-guide.md](claude-md-guide.md)** — How to write a good CLAUDE.md (architecture, constraints, build commands)\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Internal vs Public Projects\n33\t\n34\t**CRITICAL DISTINCTION**: README purpose differs based on audience and\n35\tproject type.\n36\t\n37\t### Project Types\n38\t\n39\t**Internal Projects** (always multi-service):\n40\t\n41\t-   Multi-service monorepos containing business logic\\\n42\t-   Examples: ecommerce-platform, inventory-system, customer-portal\\\n43\t-   Private repositories\\\n44\t-   For internal team use only\n45\t\n46\t**Public Projects** (always single-purpose libraries):\n47\t\n48\t-   Single-purpose, reusable technical libraries\\\n49\t-   Examples: `github.com/bborbe/errors`, `github.com/bborbe/metrics`,\n50\t    `github.com/bborbe/time`\\\n51\t-   Published on GitHub\\\n52\t-   For external developers to adopt\n53\t\n54\t**Note**: Multi-service projects are **always internal**. You publish\n55\ttechnical libraries, not business logic.\n56\t\n57\t### README Philosophy by Project Type\n58\t\n59\t**Internal Projects** (multi-service):\n60\t\n61\t-   **Very short** (50-100 lines)\\\n62\t-   Minimal, practical reference for daily work\\\n63\t-   Only what you need daily: how to run, quick commands, links to\n64\t    docs\\\n65\t-   No \"sales pitch\" or detailed explanations\\\n66\t-   Assumes reader already knows why the project exists\n67\t\n68\t**Example**: \"Here's how to run it, here's where the docs are, done.\"\n69\t\n70\t**Public Projects** (libraries):\n71\t\n72\t-   **Comprehensive** (200-400 lines)\\\n73\t-   Marketing + documentation to attract users\\\n74\t-   Convince users the library is useful and well-maintained\\\n75\t-   Show features, benefits, examples\\\n76\t-   Include badges (build status, coverage, version)\\\n77\t-   Detailed quick start with copy-paste examples\\\n78\t-   Comparison with alternatives\n79\t\n80\t**Example**: \"Here's why this is great, here's what it does, here's how\n81\teasy it is to use.\"\n82\t\n83\t### Key Differences\n84\t\n85\t  ---\n86\t  Aspect                     Internal Projects   Public Libraries\n87\t                             (Multi-Service)     (Single-Purpose)\n88\t  -------------------------- ------------------- -------------------------\n89\t  Project Type               Multi-service       Single-purpose library\n90\t                             monorepo\n91\t\n92\t  Visibility                 Private             Public (GitHub)\n93\t\n94\t  Content                    Business logic      Reusable technical\n95\t                                                 components\n96\t\n97\t  Primary Goal               Quick reference     Attract users\n98\t\n99\t  README Length              50-100 lines        200-400 lines\n100\t\n101\t  Tone                       Terse, practical    Welcoming, explanatory\n102\t\n103\t  Examples                   Minimal             Comprehensive\n104\t\n105\t  Installation               Link to docs        Detailed steps\n106\t\n107\t  Features List              Optional            Required\n108\t\n109\t  Badges                     Rarely              Usually\n110\t\n111\t  Screenshots/Demos          Rare                Common for some libs\n112\t\n113\t  Comparison to Alternatives Never               Often helpful\n114\t  ---\n115\t\n116\t---\n117\t\n118\t## 3. What Goes in `README.md`\n119\t\n120\t### For Internal Projects (Multi-Service)\n121\t\n122\tKeep it **very short** (50-100 lines). Only daily essentials.\n123\t\n124\t**Required sections**:\n125\t\n126\t-   One-line purpose\\\n127\t-   How to run locally\\\n128\t-   Link to docs/\n129\t\n130\t**Optional sections** (only if needed daily):\n131\t\n132\t-   Common commands (build, test, deploy)\\\n133\t-   Quick configuration reference\n134\t\n135\t**Do NOT include**:\n136\t\n137\t-   Full architecture\\\n138\t-   Long explanations\\\n139\t-   Detailed API docs\\\n140\t-   Troubleshooting\\\n141\t-   Historical notes\\\n142\t-   Features list\\\n143\t-   Installation instructions (link to docs instead)\n144\t\n145\t**Template** (for individual services in monorepo):\n146\t\n147\t```markdown\n148\t# [Service Name]\n149\t\n150\t> Brief one-line description\n151\t\n152\tPart of the [Project Name](../) monorepo.\n153\t\n154\t## Running Locally\n155\t\n156\t```bash\n157\tgo run main.go\n158\t# or\n159\tmake run\n160\t```\n161\t\n162\t## Configuration\n163\t\n164\tKey environment variables:\n165\t- `SERVICE_PORT` - HTTP port (default: 8080)\n166\t- `DATABASE_URL` - Database connection string\n167\t\n168\tSee [../docs/configuration.md](../docs/configuration.md) for full details.\n169\t\n170\t## Documentation\n171\t\n172\t- [Root project docs](../docs/)\n173\t- [Service-specific docs](./docs/) (if exists)\n174\t```\n175\t\n176\t### For Public Projects (Libraries)\n177\t\n178\tMore comprehensive (200-400 lines). Convince users to adopt.\n179\t\n180\t**Required sections**:\n181\t\n182\t-   Brief description (one-liner or short paragraph)\\\n183\t-   Features (with emojis for visual appeal)\\\n184\t-   Installation (`go get`)\\\n185\t-   Quick Start with copy-paste example\\\n186\t-   Usage examples (Basic → Advanced)\\\n187\t-   Testing section (make commands)\\\n188\t-   Dependencies\\\n189\t-   License\n190\t\n191\t**Optional sections** (use when valuable):\n192\t\n193\t-   Badges (GoDoc, Go Report Card - only if you maintain them)\\\n194\t-   API Reference (inline for key functions)\\\n195\t-   Advanced Features section\\\n196\t-   Requirements (Go version)\n197\t\n198\t**Do NOT include**:\n199\t\n200\t-   Contributing guidelines (unnecessary for your libraries)\\\n201\t-   Roadmap (adds maintenance burden)\\\n202\t-   FAQ (wait until questions actually emerge)\\\n203\t-   Comparison with alternatives (unless critical)\\\n204\t-   Build status badges (unless CI is public and stable)\n205\t\n206\t**Template** (based on your actual libraries):\n207\t\n208\t```markdown\n209\t# [Library Name]\n210\t\n211\t[![Go Reference](https://pkg.go.dev/badge/github.com/bborbe/[name].svg)](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/bborbe/[name])\n212\t[![Go Report Card](https://goreportcard.com/badge/github.com/bborbe/[name])](https://goreportcard.com/report/github.com/bborbe/[name])\n213\t\n214\t[One-sentence description, or 2-3 sentence paragraph explaining purpose and value]\n215\t\n216\t## Features\n217\t\n218\t- 🎯 **Feature 1** - Brief explanation\n219\t- 💉 **Feature 2** - Brief explanation\n220\t- 📅 **Feature 3** - Brief explanation\n221\t- ✅ **Feature 4** - Brief explanation\n222\t\n223\t## Installation\n224\t\n225\t```bash\n226\tgo get github.com/bborbe/[name]\n227\t```\n228\t\n229\t## Quick Start\n230\t\n231\t```go\n232\timport \"github.com/bborbe/[name]\"\n233\t\n234\t// Simple, copy-paste ready example showing core functionality\n235\tresult := name.DoSomething()\n236\t```\n237\t\n238\t## Usage\n239\t\n240\t### Basic Usage\n241\t\n242\t```go\n243\t// Show most common use case with clear example\n244\t```\n245\t\n246\t### Advanced Usage\n247\t\n248\t```go\n249\t// Show more complex scenarios if needed\n250\t```\n251\t\n252\t## API Reference\n253\t\n254\t[Optional: Include key functions inline with signatures and examples]\n255\t\n256\t### FunctionName\n257\t```go\n258\tfunc FunctionName[T any](param T) T\n259\t```\n260\tBrief description and example.\n261\t\n262\t[For simple libraries, you can skip this and just link to GoDoc]\n263\t\n264\tComplete API documentation: [pkg.go.dev](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/bborbe/[name])\n265\t\n266\t## Testing\n267\t\n268\t```bash\n269\tmake test          # Run all tests with coverage\n270\tmake precommit     # Format, test, lint, etc.\n271\tmake generate      # Generate mocks using counterfeiter\n272\t```\n273\t\n274\t## Dependencies\n275\t\n276\t- `github.com/bborbe/errors` - Error handling\n277\t- `github.com/bborbe/run` - Application runner\n278\t[List only direct dependencies users should know about]\n279\t\n280\t## Requirements\n281\t\n282\t- Go 1.24+ or later\n283\t\n284\t## License\n285\t\n286\tBSD-style license. See [LICENSE](LICENSE) file for details.\n287\t```\n288\t\n289\t**Examples from actual libraries**:\n290\t\n291\t- **Minimal** (errors): Just title + description (4 lines)\n292\t- **Moderate** (metrics): ~70 lines with features + examples\n293\t- **Comprehensive** (time, collection, http): 200-320 lines with full examples and API reference\n294\t\n295\t---\n296\t\n297\t## 4. What Goes in `docs/` - Context Memory for AI Assistants\n298\t\n299\t**CRITICAL PURPOSE**: `docs/` serves as **context memory** for AI\n300\tassistants (Claude Code, etc.) when resuming work after months.\n301\t\n302\t**Goal**: Enable AI to quickly understand:\n303\t\n304\t-   **Business context**: Why does this component exist? What problem\n305\t    does it solve?\\\n306\t-   **Technical context**: How does it work? What patterns are used?\\\n307\t-   **Current state**: What's implemented? What's planned? What\n308\t    decisions were made?\\\n309\t-   **Operational context**: How to run, deploy, debug?\n310\t\n311\t### Recommended Structure\n312\t\n313\t    docs/\n314\t    ├── overview.md           → Business + technical overview\n315\t    ├── architecture.md       → Technical design and patterns\n316\t    ├── api.md                → API/interface documentation\n317\t    ├── configuration.md      → All configuration options\n318\t    ├── operations.md         → Running, deploying, monitoring\n319\t    ├── troubleshooting.md    → Common issues and solutions\n320\t    ├── development.md        → Development workflow and setup\n321\t    ├── prd/                  → Product requirements (features)\n322\t    │   ├── 2024-001-feature-name.md\n323\t    │   └── 2024-002-another-feature.md\n324\t    └── adr/                  → Architecture decisions\n325\t        ├── 0001-use-kafka-for-events.md\n326\t        └── 0002-postgresql-vs-scylladb.md\n327\t\n328\t### 4.1 overview.md - The Starting Point\n329\t\n330\t**Purpose**: First file AI reads to understand the component.\n331\t\n332\t**Must answer**:\n333\t\n334\t-   What is this component? (1-2 sentences)\\\n335\t-   Why does it exist? What business problem does it solve?\\\n336\t-   What are its responsibilities?\\\n337\t-   What are its dependencies (upstream/downstream)?\\\n338\t-   Where does it fit in the system?\n339\t\n340\t**Template**:\n341\t\n342\t```markdown\n343\t# [Component Name] Overview\n344\t\n345\t## Purpose\n346\t\n347\t[2-3 sentences explaining what this component does and why it exists]\n348\t\n349\t## Business Context\n350\t\n351\t- **Problem**: [What business problem does this solve?]\n352\t- **Solution**: [How does this component solve it?]\n353\t- **Value**: [Why is this important to the business?]\n354\t\n355\t## Responsibilities\n356\t\n357\t- Responsibility 1\n358\t- Responsibility 2\n359\t- Responsibility 3\n360\t\n361\t## System Context\n362\t\n363\t```\n364\t[Simple diagram showing where this component fits]\n365\tExternal API → This Service → Kafka → Downstream Services\n366\t```\n367\t\n368\t## Key Dependencies\n369\t\n370\t- **Upstream**: Services/APIs this component depends on\n371\t- **Downstream**: Services/APIs that depend on this component\n372\t- **Infrastructure**: Databases, message queues, etc.\n373\t\n374\t## Current State\n375\t\n376\t- **Status**: Production / Development / Deprecated\n377\t- **Maturity**: Stable / Active Development / Experimental\n378\t- **Last Major Update**: 2024-11-20\n379\t\n380\t## Quick Links\n381\t\n382\t- [Architecture](./architecture.md)\n383\t- [API Documentation](./api.md)\n384\t- [Operations Guide](./operations.md)\n385\t```\n386\t\n387\t### 4.2 architecture.md - Technical Design\n388\t\n389\t**Purpose**: Explain HOW the component works technically.\n390\t\n391\t**Must include**:\n392\t\n393\t-   High-level architecture diagram\\\n394\t-   Key design patterns used\\\n395\t-   Data flow (how data moves through the system)\\\n396\t-   Internal components and their interactions\\\n397\t-   Technology choices and why\n398\t\n399\t**Template**:\n400\t\n401\t```markdown\n402\t# Architecture\n403\t\n404\t## Design Philosophy\n405\t\n406\t[1-2 paragraphs explaining the design approach and principles]\n407\t\n408\t## Component Architecture\n409\t\n410\t```\n411\t[Diagram showing internal components]\n412\tHTTP Handler → Service Layer → Repository → Database\n413\t     ↓\n414\t  Kafka Producer\n415\t```\n416\t\n417\t## Key Patterns\n418\t\n419\t- **Pattern 1**: [e.g., Repository Pattern] - Used for data access abstraction\n420\t- **Pattern 2**: [e.g., Factory Pattern] - Used for creating complex objects\n421\t- **Pattern 3**: [e.g., Command Pattern] - Used for event processing\n422\t\n423\t## Data Flow\n424\t\n425\t### Request Flow\n426\t1. HTTP request arrives at handler\n427\t2. Handler validates input\n428\t3. Service layer processes business logic\n429\t4. Repository persists data\n430\t5. Event published to Kafka\n431\t\n432\t### Event Processing Flow\n433\t[Explain how events are consumed and processed]\n434\t\n435\t## Technology Stack\n436\t\n437\t- **Language**: Go 1.24\n438\t- **Database**: PostgreSQL 15\n439\t- **Message Queue**: Kafka (Strimzi)\n440\t- **Metrics**: Prometheus\n441\t- **Logging**: Structured JSON logs\n442\t\n443\t## Key Decisions\n444\t\n445\tSee [ADRs](./adr/) for detailed architectural decisions:\n446\t- [ADR-0001: Use Kafka for events](./adr/0001-kafka-events.md)\n447\t- [ADR-0002: PostgreSQL for state](./adr/0002-postgresql.md)\n448\t```\n449\t\n450\t### 4.3 api.md - Interface Documentation\n451\t\n452\t**Purpose**: Document all interfaces (HTTP, gRPC, Kafka topics).\n453\t\n454\t**Must include**:\n455\t\n456\t-   All endpoints/topics with examples\\\n457\t-   Request/response schemas\\\n458\t-   Validation rules\\\n459\t-   Error responses\n460\t\n461\t**Example**:\n462\t\n463\t```markdown\n464\t# API Documentation\n465\t\n466\t## HTTP Endpoints\n467\t\n468\t### POST /api/orders\n469\t\n470\tCreate a new order.\n471\t\n472\t**Request**:\n473\t```json\n474\t{\n475\t  \"customer_id\": \"cust_123\",\n476\t  \"items\": [\n477\t    {\"product_id\": \"prod_456\", \"quantity\": 2}\n478\t  ]\n479\t}\n480\t```\n481\t\n482\t**Response** (201 Created):\n483\t```json\n484\t{\n485\t  \"order_id\": \"ord_789\",\n486\t  \"status\": \"pending\",\n487\t  \"created_at\": \"2024-11-27T10:00:00Z\"\n488\t}\n489\t```\n490\t\n491\t**Errors**:\n492\t- 400: Invalid request (missing customer_id)\n493\t- 404: Customer not found\n494\t- 500: Internal server error\n495\t\n496\t### GET /api/orders/:id\n497\t\n498\t[Document other endpoints...]\n499\t\n500\t## Kafka Topics\n501\t\n502\t### Produces To\n503\t\n504\t**Topic**: `orders.created`\n505\t\n506\t**Schema**:\n507\t```json\n508\t{\n509\t  \"order_id\": \"string\",\n510\t  \"customer_id\": \"string\",\n511\t  \"created_at\": \"timestamp\"\n512\t}\n513\t```\n514\t\n515\t### Consumes From\n516\t\n517\t**Topic**: `payments.completed`\n518\t\n519\t**Handling**: Updates order status to \"paid\"\n520\t```\n521\t\n522\t### 4.4 configuration.md - All Configuration\n523\t\n524\t**Purpose**: Document every environment variable and config option.\n525\t\n526\t**Must include**:\n527\t\n528\t-   All ENV vars with types and defaults\\\n529\t-   Required vs optional\\\n530\t-   Examples for different environments\n531\t\n532\t**Example**:\n533\t\n534\t```markdown\n535\t# Configuration\n536\t\n537\t## Environment Variables\n538\t\n539\t### Required\n540\t\n541\t- `DATABASE_URL` (string) - PostgreSQL connection string\n542\t  - Example: `postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/dbname`\n543\t- `KAFKA_BROKERS` (string) - Comma-separated Kafka brokers\n544\t  - Example: `kafka-1:9092,kafka-2:9092`\n545\t\n546\t### Optional\n547\t\n548\t- `PORT` (int) - HTTP server port\n549\t  - Default: `8080`\n550\t- `LOG_LEVEL` (string) - Logging level\n551\t  - Default: `info`\n552\t  - Options: `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`\n553\t- `MAX_CONNECTIONS` (int) - Database connection pool size\n554\t  - Default: `10`\n555\t\n556\t## Configuration Files\n557\t\n558\t[If using config files, document structure here]\n559\t\n560\t## Example Configurations\n561\t\n562\t### Development\n563\t```bash\n564\tDATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost:5432/dev\n565\tKAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:9092\n566\tLOG_LEVEL=debug\n567\t```\n568\t\n569\t### Production\n570\t```bash\n571\tDATABASE_URL=postgresql://prod-db:5432/orders\n572\tKAFKA_BROKERS=kafka-1:9092,kafka-2:9092,kafka-3:9092\n573\tLOG_LEVEL=info\n574\tMAX_CONNECTIONS=50\n575\t```\n576\t```\n577\t\n578\t### 4.5 operations.md - Running and Deploying\n579\t\n580\t**Purpose**: How to operate this component in production.\n581\t\n582\t**Must include**:\n583\t\n584\t-   How to deploy\\\n585\t-   How to monitor\\\n586\t-   How to rollback\\\n587\t-   Key metrics and alerts\n588\t\n589\t**Example**:\n590\t\n591\t```markdown\n592\t# Operations Guide\n593\t\n594\t## Deployment\n595\t\n596\t### Prerequisites\n597\t- Kubernetes cluster with access\n598\t- Database migrations applied\n599\t- Kafka topics created\n600\t\n601\t### Deploy to Dev\n602\t```bash\n603\tmake deploy-dev\n604\t# or\n605\tkubectl apply -f k8s/dev/\n606\t```\n607\t\n608\t### Deploy to Production\n609\t```bash\n610\t# Check current version\n611\tkubectlprod get deployment order-service\n612\t\n613\t# Deploy new version\n614\tmake deploy-prod VERSION=v1.2.3\n615\t\n616\t# Monitor rollout\n617\tkubectlprod rollout status deployment/order-service\n618\t```\n619\t\n620\t## Monitoring\n621\t\n622\t### Key Metrics\n623\t- `orders_created_total` - Total orders created\n624\t- `orders_failed_total` - Failed order creations\n625\t- `order_processing_duration_seconds` - Processing time\n626\t\n627\t### Alerts\n628\t- **High Error Rate**: >5% errors in 5 minutes\n629\t- **High Latency**: p99 >1s for 5 minutes\n630\t- **Low Throughput**: <10 orders/minute\n631\t\n632\t### Dashboards\n633\t- [Grafana: Order Service Overview](https://grafana.example.com/d/orders)\n634\t\n635\t## Rollback\n636\t\n637\t```bash\n638\t# Rollback to previous version\n639\tkubectlprod rollout undo deployment/order-service\n640\t\n641\t# Rollback to specific version\n642\tkubectlprod rollout undo deployment/order-service --to-revision=5\n643\t```\n644\t\n645\t## Health Checks\n646\t\n647\t- `/healthz` - Liveness probe (always returns 200)\n648\t- `/readyz` - Readiness probe (checks DB + Kafka connectivity)\n649\t```\n650\t\n651\t### 4.6 troubleshooting.md - Common Issues\n652\t\n653\t**Purpose**: Known issues and how to fix them.\n654\t\n655\t**Must include**:\n656\t\n657\t-   Common errors with solutions\\\n658\t-   Debug procedures\\\n659\t-   FAQ\n660\t\n661\t**Example**:\n662\t\n663\t```markdown\n664\t# Troubleshooting\n665\t\n666\t## Common Issues\n667\t\n668\t### Database Connection Timeout\n669\t\n670\t**Symptom**: `pq: connection timeout` errors in logs\n671\t\n672\t**Cause**: Database connection pool exhausted\n673\t\n674\t**Solution**:\n675\t1. Check current connections: `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;`\n676\t2. Increase `MAX_CONNECTIONS` environment variable\n677\t3. Check for connection leaks in code\n678\t\n679\t### Kafka Consumer Lag\n680\t\n681\t**Symptom**: Consumer lag increasing, events not processed\n682\t\n683\t**Cause**: Processing too slow or consumer crashed\n684\t\n685\t**Solution**:\n686\t1. Check consumer group lag: `kafka-consumer-groups --describe --group order-service`\n687\t2. Scale up consumer replicas\n688\t3. Check for errors in processing logic\n689\t\n690\t## Debug Procedures\n691\t\n692\t### Enable Debug Logging\n693\t```bash\n694\t# Temporarily enable debug logging\n695\tkubectlprod set env deployment/order-service LOG_LEVEL=debug\n696\t\n697\t# Remember to revert after debugging\n698\tkubectlprod set env deployment/order-service LOG_LEVEL=info\n699\t```\n700\t\n701\t### Access Logs\n702\t```bash\n703\t# View recent logs\n704\tkubectlprod logs deployment/order-service --tail=100\n705\t\n706\t# Follow logs in real-time\n707\tkubectlprod logs deployment/order-service -f\n708\t\n709\t# Filter for errors\n710\tkubectlprod logs deployment/order-service | grep ERROR\n711\t```\n712\t```\n713\t\n714\t### 4.7 development.md - Development Workflow\n715\t\n716\t**Purpose**: How to work on this component locally.\n717\t\n718\t**Must include**:\n719\t\n720\t-   Local setup steps\\\n721\t-   How to run tests\\\n722\t-   Development workflow\\\n723\t-   Code organization\n724\t\n725\t**Example**:\n726\t\n727\t```markdown\n728\t# Development Guide\n729\t\n730\t## Local Setup\n731\t\n732\t### Prerequisites\n733\t- Go 1.24+\n734\t- Docker and Docker Compose\n735\t- Make\n736\t\n737\t### Setup\n738\t```bash\n739\t# Clone and install dependencies\n740\tgit clone [repo]\n741\tcd order-service\n742\tgo mod download\n743\t\n744\t# Start dependencies (DB, Kafka)\n745\tdocker-compose up -d\n746\t\n747\t# Run migrations\n748\tmake migrate\n749\t\n750\t# Run service\n751\tmake run\n752\t```\n753\t\n754\t## Running Tests\n755\t\n756\t```bash\n757\t# Run all tests\n758\tmake test\n759\t\n760\t# Run with coverage\n761\tmake test-coverage\n762\t\n763\t# Run integration tests\n764\tmake test-integration\n765\t```\n766\t\n767\t## Code Organization\n768\t\n769\t```\n770\torder-service/\n771\t├── cmd/            - Main application entry\n772\t├── internal/       - Private application code\n773\t│   ├── handler/   - HTTP handlers\n774\t│   ├── service/   - Business logic\n775\t│   ├── repository/- Data access\n776\t│   └── model/     - Domain models\n777\t├── pkg/           - Public libraries\n778\t└── docs/          - Documentation\n779\t```\n780\t\n781\t## Development Workflow\n782\t\n783\t1. Create feature branch from `master`\n784\t2. Make changes and write tests\n785\t3. Run `make precommit` (format, test, lint)\n786\t4. Commit and push\n787\t5. Create pull request\n788\t```\n789\t\n790\t---\n791\t\n792\t## 5. Product Requirement Definitions (PRDs)\n793\t\n794\t**Purpose**: Document features BEFORE implementation to provide context for AI assistants.\n795\t\n796\t**Full documentation**: See [prd-guide.md](prd-guide.md) for complete PRD templates, examples, and best practices.\n797\t\n798\t**Key points**:\n799\t- **Stored in**: `docs/prd/`\n800\t- **Naming**: `YYYY-NNN-feature-name.md` (e.g., `2024-001-order-cancellation.md`)\n801\t- **When to create**: Feature requires >1 day of work, multiple approaches possible, affects multiple components\n802\t- **Status tracking**: Draft → In Progress → Implemented → Deprecated\n803\t\n804\t**PRD Workflow**: For AI-assisted PRD creation and implementation workflow, see project-local PRD workflow guide.\n805\t\n806\tFor complete template with all sections, examples, and detailed guidance, see [prd-guide.md](prd-guide.md).\n807\t\n808\t---\n809\t\n810\t## 6. Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)\n811\t\n812\t**Purpose**: Document important architectural decisions and their rationale for future reference.\n813\t\n814\t**Full documentation**: See [adr-guide.md](adr-guide.md) for complete ADR templates, examples, and best practices.\n815\t\n816\t**Key points**:\n817\t- **Stored in**: `docs/adr/` (root-level) or `service-name/docs/adr/` (service-specific)\n818\t- **Naming**: `NNNN-decision-title.md` (e.g., `0001-use-kafka-for-events.md`)\n819\t- **When to create**: Technology choices, architectural patterns, decisions affecting multiple components, expensive-to-reverse decisions\n820\t- **Status tracking**: Proposed → Accepted → Deprecated/Superseded\n821\t\n822\t**Organization**:\n823\t- **Root-level ADRs** (`docs/adr/`): Project-wide decisions affecting multiple services\n824\t- **Service-level ADRs** (`service-name/docs/adr/`): Service-specific architectural decisions\n825\t\n826\tFor complete template, examples, organization guidance, and best practices, see [adr-guide.md](adr-guide.md).\n827\t\n828\t---\n829\t\n830\t## 7. When to Add Documentation\n831\t\n832\tAdd docs when: - A change affects users or APIs\\\n833\t- Multiple people work on the feature\\\n834\t- It's larger than 1--2 tickets\\\n835\t- A decision has long-term impact\\\n836\t- A problem might repeat\n837\t\n838\t---\n839\t\n840\t## 8. Writing for AI Context Resumption\n841\t\n842\t**Critical Principle**: Write documentation so an AI assistant (Claude\n843\tCode, etc.) can understand the project state after months of absence.\n844\t\n845\t### What AI Assistants Need\n846\t\n847\t**Business Context**:\n848\t\n849\t-   WHY does this exist?\\\n850\t-   What problem are we solving?\\\n851\t-   Who are the users?\\\n852\t-   What's the business value?\n853\t\n854\t**Technical Context**:\n855\t\n856\t-   HOW does it work?\\\n857\t-   What patterns are used?\\\n858\t-   What technologies are involved?\\\n859\t-   How do components interact?\n860\t\n861\t**Current State**:\n862\t\n863\t-   What's implemented vs planned?\\\n864\t-   What decisions were made?\\\n865\t-   What's working vs broken?\\\n866\t-   What are known limitations?\n867\t\n868\t**Operational Context**:\n869\t\n870\t-   How to run locally?\\\n871\t-   How to deploy?\\\n872\t-   How to debug?\\\n873\t-   What's being monitored?\n874\t\n875\t### Writing Best Practices\n876\t\n877\t**Assume Zero Prior Knowledge**:\n878\t\n879\t```markdown\n880\t❌ Bad: \"Use the standard deployment process\"\n881\t✓ Good: \"Deploy using `make deploy-prod`, which builds the Docker image,\n882\tpushes to GCR, and applies Kubernetes manifests\"\n883\t\n884\t❌ Bad: \"Configure the usual environment variables\"\n885\t✓ Good: \"Required environment variables: DATABASE_URL (PostgreSQL\n886\tconnection), KAFKA_BROKERS (comma-separated list)\"\n887\t```\n888\t\n889\t**Explain WHY, Not Just WHAT**:\n890\t\n891\t```markdown\n892\t❌ Bad: \"We use PostgreSQL for storage\"\n893\t✓ Good: \"We use PostgreSQL for storage because we need ACID transactions\n894\tfor order processing and strong consistency guarantees\"\n895\t```\n896\t\n897\t**Include Diagrams for System Understanding**:\n898\t\n899\t```markdown\n900\t✓ Good:\n901\t```text\n902\tUser Request → API Gateway → Order Service → PostgreSQL\n903\t                                   ↓\n904\t                              Kafka (orders.created)\n905\t                                   ↓\n906\t                            Notification Service\n907\t```\n908\t```\n909\t\n910\t**Document Current State**:\n911\t\n912\t```markdown\n913\t✓ Good:\n914\t## Current State (2024-11-27)\n915\t\n916\t**Implemented**:\n917\t- Order creation and retrieval\n918\t- Payment processing integration\n919\t- Email notifications\n920\t\n921\t**In Progress**:\n922\t- Order cancellation (see PRD-2024-003)\n923\t- Refund processing\n924\t\n925\t**Known Issues**:\n926\t- High latency on order search (>2s) - investigating database indexes\n927\t- Occasional Kafka consumer lag during peak hours\n928\t\n929\t**Planned**:\n930\t- Partial cancellations (Q1 2025)\n931\t- Subscription orders (Q2 2025)\n932\t```\n933\t\n934\t**Keep Documentation Updated**:\n935\t\n936\t```markdown\n937\t✓ Good: Add update log to long-lived docs\n938\t\n939\t## Updates Log\n940\t- 2024-11-27: Added cancellation endpoint documentation\n941\t- 2024-10-15: Updated deployment process for new Kubernetes cluster\n942\t- 2024-09-01: Initial version\n943\t```\n944\t\n945\t**Link Related Documentation**:\n946\t\n947\t```markdown\n948\t✓ Good:\n949\tThis order cancellation feature (PRD-2024-003) requires:\n950\t- [Payment refund integration](./integration/payment-service.md)\n951\t- [Kafka event schema update](../adr/0015-order-event-schema.md)\n952\t- [Database migration](./operations.md#migrations)\n953\t```\n954\t\n955\t---\n956\t\n957\t## 9. Style Rules\n958\t\n959\t### Do:\n960\t\n961\t-   Keep files focused\\\n962\t-   Use examples\\\n963\t-   Link between docs (use relative links)\\\n964\t-   Use diagrams when helpful\\\n965\t-   Keep docs up-to-date (remove outdated content)\\\n966\t-   Version diagrams with dates or git commits\\\n967\t-   **Write for AI assistants**: Assume reader has no prior knowledge\\\n968\t-   **Explain WHY**: Not just what was done, but why it was done that\n969\t    way\\\n970\t-   **Document current state**: What works, what's broken, what's\n971\t    planned\n972\t\n973\t### Don't:\n974\t\n975\t-   Duplicate content\\\n976\t-   Write unnecessarily long text\\\n977\t-   Document obvious code patterns (let GoDoc handle that)\\\n978\t-   Create extensive docs for every tiny component\\\n979\t-   **Assume prior knowledge**: AI has no memory of previous work\\\n980\t-   **Skip business context**: Technical details without business\n981\t    reasoning\\\n982\t-   **Leave docs stale**: Outdated docs are worse than no docs\n983\t\n984\t---\n985\t\n986\t## 10. Multi-Service Projects / Monorepos\n987\t\n988\tFor projects containing multiple services (like `ecommerce-platform`, `inventory-system`):\n989\t\n990\t### Repository Root Structure\n991\t\n992\t    README.md           → High-level overview of entire project\n993\t    docs/               → Project-wide documentation\n994\t    ├── architecture.md → Overall system architecture\n995\t    ├── development.md  → Development workflows, tooling\n996\t    ├── deployment.md   → Project-wide deployment procedures\n997\t    ├── adr/           → Cross-service architectural decisions\n998\t    └── prd/           → Project-wide features\n999\t    <service-a>/\n1000\t    ├── README.md      → Service-specific quickstart\n1001\t    ├── docs/          → Service-specific docs (optional)\n1002\t    <service-b>/\n1003\t    ├── README.md      → Service-specific quickstart\n1004\t    └── docs/          → Service-specific docs (optional)\n1005\t\n1006\t### Root README.md\n1007\t\n1008\tThe root README provides project-wide orientation.\n1009\t\n1010\tMulti-service projects are **always internal**, so keep it minimal\n1011\t(50-100 lines).\n1012\t\n1013\t**Required**:\n1014\t\n1015\t-   Brief project purpose\\\n1016\t-   List of key services (or link to service list)\\\n1017\t-   Essential daily commands\\\n1018\t-   Link to docs/\n1019\t\n1020\t**Template**:\n1021\t\n1022\t```markdown\n1023\t# [Project Name]\n1024\t\n1025\t> Brief one-line description\n1026\t\n1027\t[Optional: One paragraph context if needed]\n1028\t\n1029\t## Services\n1030\t\n1031\tSee [docs/services.md](./docs/services.md) for full service list.\n1032\t\n1033\tKey services:\n1034\t- [service-name](./service-name/) - Brief description\n1035\t- [service-name](./service-name/) - Brief description\n1036\t- [service-name](./service-name/) - Brief description\n1037\t\n1038\t## Quick Commands\n1039\t\n1040\t```bash\n1041\t# Run all tests\n1042\tmake test\n1043\t\n1044\t# Update all dependencies\n1045\tmake gomodupdate\n1046\t\n1047\t# Precommit checks\n1048\tmake precommit\n1049\t\n1050\t# Deploy to dev\n1051\tmake deploy-dev\n1052\t\n1053\t# Deploy to prod\n1054\tmake deploy-prod\n1055\t```\n1056\t\n1057\t## Documentation\n1058\t\n1059\tSee [docs/](./docs/) for detailed documentation:\n1060\t\n1061\t- [Architecture](./docs/architecture.md)\n1062\t- [Development Guide](./docs/development.md)\n1063\t- [Deployment](./docs/deployment.md)\n1064\t```\n1065\t\n1066\t### Root docs/\n1067\t\n1068\tProject-wide documentation that affects multiple services.\n1069\t\n1070\t-   Cross-service architecture diagrams\\\n1071\t-   Shared infrastructure (Kafka, databases, monitoring)\\\n1072\t-   Development workflows (commit process, testing, CI/CD)\\\n1073\t-   Deployment procedures affecting multiple services\\\n1074\t-   Common patterns used across services\n1075\t\n1076\t### Service-Level README.md\n1077\t\n1078\t**Each service MUST have its own README.md** following the standard structure (Section 2).\n1079\t\n1080\t-   Overview of the specific service\\\n1081\t-   How to run this service locally\\\n1082\t-   Service-specific configuration\\\n1083\t-   Link to root docs/ for shared infrastructure\n1084\t\n1085\t### Service-Level docs/\n1086\t\n1087\t**IMPORTANT**: Most services should have their own `docs/` folder for\n1088\tAI context.\n1089\t\n1090\t**Purpose**: Provide comprehensive context about THIS specific service\n1091\tfor AI assistants.\n1092\t\n1093\t**When to create** (generally always for non-trivial services):\n1094\t\n1095\t-   Service has unique business logic\\\n1096\t-   Service has its own API or interfaces\\\n1097\t-   Service has specific operational concerns\\\n1098\t-   **AI needs context to work on this service**\n1099\t\n1100\t**Recommended structure** (use Section 4 templates):\n1101\t\n1102\t``` markdown\n1103\tservice-name/\n1104\t├── README.md              → Minimal quickstart (50-100 lines)\n1105\t└── docs/\n1106\t    ├── overview.md        → Business + technical overview\n1107\t    ├── architecture.md    → How this service works\n1108\t    ├── api.md             → Service-specific API/interfaces\n1109\t    ├── configuration.md   → Environment variables\n1110\t    ├── operations.md      → Deploy, monitor, troubleshoot\n1111\t    ├── prd/              → Service-specific features\n1112\t    └── adr/              → Service-specific decisions\n1113\t```\n1114\t\n1115\t**Key principle**: README stays minimal, `docs/` contains all the\n1116\tcontext AI needs.\n1117\t\n1118\t### When to Document at Root vs Service Level\n1119\t\n1120\t  ---\n1121\t  Root docs/                    Service docs/\n1122\t  ----------------------------- ------------------------------------------\n1123\t  Cross-service data flow       Service-specific API endpoints\n1124\t\n1125\t  Shared Kafka topics           Service-unique business logic\n1126\t\n1127\t  Common deployment process     Service-specific troubleshooting\n1128\t\n1129\t  Shared monitoring setup       Service-unique configuration\n1130\t\n1131\t  Project-wide ADRs             Service-specific decisions\n1132\t  ---\n1133\t\n1134\t---\n1135\t\n1136\t## Summary Table\n1137\t\n1138\t  ---\n1139\t  File / Folder             Purpose\n1140\t  ------------------------- ----------------------------------------------\n1141\t  README.md                 Quickstart + high-level overview\n1142\t\n1143\t  docs/                     Detailed technical documentation\n1144\t\n1145\t  docs/prd/                 Product Requirement Definitions\n1146\t\n1147\t  docs/adr/                 Long-term architectural decisions\n1148\t\n1149\t  docs/api.md               Full interface and endpoint documentation\n1150\t\n1151\t  docs/architecture.md      Internal design and system structure\n1152\t\n1153\t  docs/configuration.md     Full configuration reference\n1154\t\n1155\t  docs/operations.md        Deployment and operations\n1156\t\n1157\t  docs/troubleshooting.md   Known issues and fixes\n1158\t  ---\n1159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79165e9f-87b0-4e96-8a77-dd19caa6f4f6","uuid":"3e18a4ab-86e7-4c85-9074-8c957e849ba1","timestamp":"2026-09-03T19:17:13.138Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/documentation-guide.md","content":"# Documentation Guide\n\n## 1. Overview of Documentation Structure\n\nEach project uses three layers:\n\n    README.md        → What it does, install, use (for humans on GitHub)\n    CLAUDE.md        → How to change the code safely (for AI agents)\n    docs/            → Detailed and in-depth documentation (for both)\n    docs/adr/        → (Optional) Architecture Decision Records\n    docs/prd/        → Product Requirement Definitions\n\n### What Goes Where\n\n| Content | README.md | CLAUDE.md | docs/ |\n|---------|-----------|-----------|-------|\n| What the project does | Yes | 1 line only | Overview |\n| Install/usage | Yes | Never | - |\n| Features/marketing | Yes | Never | - |\n| Build/test commands | Brief | Detailed | - |\n| Architecture | Never | Package map | Full detail |\n| Design decisions | Never | Constraints list | ADRs |\n| Env vars/config | User-facing | Agent-needs | Full reference |\n| API docs | Brief | Never | Full detail |\n| Workflow rules | Never | Yes | - |\n\n### Detailed Guides\n\n- **[readme-guide.md](readme-guide.md)** — How to write a good README.md (templates, sections, project types)\n- **[claude-md-guide.md](claude-md-guide.md)** — How to write a good CLAUDE.md (architecture, constraints, build commands)\n\n## 2. Internal vs Public Projects\n\n**CRITICAL DISTINCTION**: README purpose differs based on audience and\nproject type.\n\n### Project Types\n\n**Internal Projects** (always multi-service):\n\n-   Multi-service monorepos containing business logic\\\n-   Examples: ecommerce-platform, inventory-system, customer-portal\\\n-   Private repositories\\\n-   For internal team use only\n\n**Public Projects** (always single-purpose libraries):\n\n-   Single-purpose, reusable technical libraries\\\n-   Examples: `github.com/bborbe/errors`, `github.com/bborbe/metrics`,\n    `github.com/bborbe/time`\\\n-   Published on GitHub\\\n-   For external developers to adopt\n\n**Note**: Multi-service projects are **always internal**. You publish\ntechnical libraries, not business logic.\n\n### README Philosophy by Project Type\n\n**Internal Projects** (multi-service):\n\n-   **Very short** (50-100 lines)\\\n-   Minimal, practical reference for daily work\\\n-   Only what you need daily: how to run, quick commands, links to\n    docs\\\n-   No \"sales pitch\" or detailed explanations\\\n-   Assumes reader already knows why the project exists\n\n**Example**: \"Here's how to run it, here's where the docs are, done.\"\n\n**Public Projects** (libraries):\n\n-   **Comprehensive** (200-400 lines)\\\n-   Marketing + documentation to attract users\\\n-   Convince users the library is useful and well-maintained\\\n-   Show features, benefits, examples\\\n-   Include badges (build status, coverage, version)\\\n-   Detailed quick start with copy-paste examples\\\n-   Comparison with alternatives\n\n**Example**: \"Here's why this is great, here's what it does, here's how\neasy it is to use.\"\n\n### Key Differences\n\n  ---\n  Aspect                     Internal Projects   Public Libraries\n                             (Multi-Service)     (Single-Purpose)\n  -------------------------- ------------------- -------------------------\n  Project Type               Multi-service       Single-purpose library\n                             monorepo\n\n  Visibility                 Private             Public (GitHub)\n\n  Content                    Business logic      Reusable technical\n                                                 components\n\n  Primary Goal               Quick reference     Attract users\n\n  README Length              50-100 lines        200-400 lines\n\n  Tone                       Terse, practical    Welcoming, explanatory\n\n  Examples                   Minimal             Comprehensive\n\n  Installation               Link to docs        Detailed steps\n\n  Features List              Optional            Required\n\n  Badges                     Rarely              Usually\n\n  Screenshots/Demos          Rare                Common for some libs\n\n  Comparison to Alternatives Never               Often helpful\n  ---\n\n---\n\n## 3. What Goes in `README.md`\n\n### For Internal Projects (Multi-Service)\n\nKeep it **very short** (50-100 lines). Only daily essentials.\n\n**Required sections**:\n\n-   One-line purpose\\\n-   How to run locally\\\n-   Link to docs/\n\n**Optional sections** (only if needed daily):\n\n-   Common commands (build, test, deploy)\\\n-   Quick configuration reference\n\n**Do NOT include**:\n\n-   Full architecture\\\n-   Long explanations\\\n-   Detailed API docs\\\n-   Troubleshooting\\\n-   Historical notes\\\n-   Features list\\\n-   Installation instructions (link to docs instead)\n\n**Template** (for individual services in monorepo):\n\n```markdown\n# [Service Name]\n\n> Brief one-line description\n\nPart of the [Project Name](../) monorepo.\n\n## Running Locally\n\n```bash\ngo run main.go\n# or\nmake run\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nKey environment variables:\n- `SERVICE_PORT` - HTTP port (default: 8080)\n- `DATABASE_URL` - Database connection string\n\nSee [../docs/configuration.md](../docs/configuration.md) for full details.\n\n## Documentation\n\n- [Root project docs](../docs/)\n- [Service-specific docs](./docs/) (if exists)\n```\n\n### For Public Projects (Libraries)\n\nMore comprehensive (200-400 lines). Convince users to adopt.\n\n**Required sections**:\n\n-   Brief description (one-liner or short paragraph)\\\n-   Features (with emojis for visual appeal)\\\n-   Installation (`go get`)\\\n-   Quick Start with copy-paste example\\\n-   Usage examples (Basic → Advanced)\\\n-   Testing section (make commands)\\\n-   Dependencies\\\n-   License\n\n**Optional sections** (use when valuable):\n\n-   Badges (GoDoc, Go Report Card - only if you maintain them)\\\n-   API Reference (inline for key functions)\\\n-   Advanced Features section\\\n-   Requirements (Go version)\n\n**Do NOT include**:\n\n-   Contributing guidelines (unnecessary for your libraries)\\\n-   Roadmap (adds maintenance burden)\\\n-   FAQ (wait until questions actually emerge)\\\n-   Comparison with alternatives (unless critical)\\\n-   Build status badges (unless CI is public and stable)\n\n**Template** (based on your actual libraries):\n\n```markdown\n# [Library Name]\n\n[![Go Reference](https://pkg.go.dev/badge/github.com/bborbe/[name].svg)](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/bborbe/[name])\n[![Go Report Card](https://goreportcard.com/badge/github.com/bborbe/[name])](https://goreportcard.com/report/github.com/bborbe/[name])\n\n[One-sentence description, or 2-3 sentence paragraph explaining purpose and value]\n\n## Features\n\n- 🎯 **Feature 1** - Brief explanation\n- 💉 **Feature 2** - Brief explanation\n- 📅 **Feature 3** - Brief explanation\n- ✅ **Feature 4** - Brief explanation\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/bborbe/[name]\n```\n\n## Quick Start\n\n```go\nimport \"github.com/bborbe/[name]\"\n\n// Simple, copy-paste ready example showing core functionality\nresult := name.DoSomething()\n```\n\n## Usage\n\n### Basic Usage\n\n```go\n// Show most common use case with clear example\n```\n\n### Advanced Usage\n\n```go\n// Show more complex scenarios if needed\n```\n\n## API Reference\n\n[Optional: Include key functions inline with signatures and examples]\n\n### FunctionName\n```go\nfunc FunctionName[T any](param T) T\n```\nBrief description and example.\n\n[For simple libraries, you can skip this and just link to GoDoc]\n\nComplete API documentation: [pkg.go.dev](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/bborbe/[name])\n\n## Testing\n\n```bash\nmake test          # Run all tests with coverage\nmake precommit     # Format, test, lint, etc.\nmake generate      # Generate mocks using counterfeiter\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- `github.com/bborbe/errors` - Error handling\n- `github.com/bborbe/run` - Application runner\n[List only direct dependencies users should know about]\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Go 1.24+ or later\n\n## License\n\nBSD-style license. See [LICENSE](LICENSE) file for details.\n```\n\n**Examples from actual libraries**:\n\n- **Minimal** (errors): Just title + description (4 lines)\n- **Moderate** (metrics): ~70 lines with features + examples\n- **Comprehensive** (time, collection, http): 200-320 lines with full examples and API reference\n\n---\n\n## 4. What Goes in `docs/` - Context Memory for AI Assistants\n\n**CRITICAL PURPOSE**: `docs/` serves as **context memory** for AI\nassistants (Claude Code, etc.) when resuming work after months.\n\n**Goal**: Enable AI to quickly understand:\n\n-   **Business context**: Why does this component exist? What problem\n    does it solve?\\\n-   **Technical context**: How does it work? What patterns are used?\\\n-   **Current state**: What's implemented? What's planned? What\n    decisions were made?\\\n-   **Operational context**: How to run, deploy, debug?\n\n### Recommended Structure\n\n    docs/\n    ├── overview.md           → Business + technical overview\n    ├── architecture.md       → Technical design and patterns\n    ├── api.md                → API/interface documentation\n    ├── configuration.md      → All configuration options\n    ├── operations.md         → Running, deploying, monitoring\n    ├── troubleshooting.md    → Common issues and solutions\n    ├── development.md        → Development workflow and setup\n    ├── prd/                  → Product requirements (features)\n    │   ├── 2024-001-feature-name.md\n    │   └── 2024-002-another-feature.md\n    └── adr/                  → Architecture decisions\n        ├── 0001-use-kafka-for-events.md\n        └── 0002-postgresql-vs-scylladb.md\n\n### 4.1 overview.md - The Starting Point\n\n**Purpose**: First file AI reads to understand the component.\n\n**Must answer**:\n\n-   What is this component? (1-2 sentences)\\\n-   Why does it exist? What business problem does it solve?\\\n-   What are its responsibilities?\\\n-   What are its dependencies (upstream/downstream)?\\\n-   Where does it fit in the system?\n\n**Template**:\n\n```markdown\n# [Component Name] Overview\n\n## Purpose\n\n[2-3 sentences explaining what this component does and why it exists]\n\n## Business Context\n\n- **Problem**: [What business problem does this solve?]\n- **Solution**: [How does this component solve it?]\n- **Value**: [Why is this important to the business?]\n\n## Responsibilities\n\n- Responsibility 1\n- Responsibility 2\n- Responsibility 3\n\n## System Context\n\n```\n[Simple diagram showing where this component fits]\nExternal API → This Service → Kafka → Downstream Services\n```\n\n## Key Dependencies\n\n- **Upstream**: Services/APIs this component depends on\n- **Downstream**: Services/APIs that depend on this component\n- **Infrastructure**: Databases, message queues, etc.\n\n## Current State\n\n- **Status**: Production / Development / Deprecated\n- **Maturity**: Stable / Active Development / Experimental\n- **Last Major Update**: 2024-11-20\n\n## Quick Links\n\n- [Architecture](./architecture.md)\n- [API Documentation](./api.md)\n- [Operations Guide](./operations.md)\n```\n\n### 4.2 architecture.md - Technical Design\n\n**Purpose**: Explain HOW the component works technically.\n\n**Must include**:\n\n-   High-level architecture diagram\\\n-   Key design patterns used\\\n-   Data flow (how data moves through the system)\\\n-   Internal components and their interactions\\\n-   Technology choices and why\n\n**Template**:\n\n```markdown\n# Architecture\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n[1-2 paragraphs explaining the design approach and principles]\n\n## Component Architecture\n\n```\n[Diagram showing internal components]\nHTTP Handler → Service Layer → Repository → Database\n     ↓\n  Kafka Producer\n```\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n- **Pattern 1**: [e.g., Repository Pattern] - Used for data access abstraction\n- **Pattern 2**: [e.g., Factory Pattern] - Used for creating complex objects\n- **Pattern 3**: [e.g., Command Pattern] - Used for event processing\n\n## Data Flow\n\n### Request Flow\n1. HTTP request arrives at handler\n2. Handler validates input\n3. Service layer processes business logic\n4. Repository persists data\n5. Event published to Kafka\n\n### Event Processing Flow\n[Explain how events are consumed and processed]\n\n## Technology Stack\n\n- **Language**: Go 1.24\n- **Database**: PostgreSQL 15\n- **Message Queue**: Kafka (Strimzi)\n- **Metrics**: Prometheus\n- **Logging**: Structured JSON logs\n\n## Key Decisions\n\nSee [ADRs](./adr/) for detailed architectural decisions:\n- [ADR-0001: Use Kafka for events](./adr/0001-kafka-events.md)\n- [ADR-0002: PostgreSQL for state](./adr/0002-postgresql.md)\n```\n\n### 4.3 api.md - Interface Documentation\n\n**Purpose**: Document all interfaces (HTTP, gRPC, Kafka topics).\n\n**Must include**:\n\n-   All endpoints/topics with examples\\\n-   Request/response schemas\\\n-   Validation rules\\\n-   Error responses\n\n**Example**:\n\n```markdown\n# API Documentation\n\n## HTTP Endpoints\n\n### POST /api/orders\n\nCreate a new order.\n\n**Request**:\n```json\n{\n  \"customer_id\": \"cust_123\",\n  \"items\": [\n    {\"product_id\": \"prod_456\", \"quantity\": 2}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Response** (201 Created):\n```json\n{\n  \"order_id\": \"ord_789\",\n  \"status\": \"pending\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2024-11-27T10:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\n**Errors**:\n- 400: Invalid request (missing customer_id)\n- 404: Customer not found\n- 500: Internal server error\n\n### GET /api/orders/:id\n\n[Document other endpoints...]\n\n## Kafka Topics\n\n### Produces To\n\n**Topic**: `orders.created`\n\n**Schema**:\n```json\n{\n  \"order_id\": \"string\",\n  \"customer_id\": \"string\",\n  \"created_at\": \"timestamp\"\n}\n```\n\n### Consumes From\n\n**Topic**: `payments.completed`\n\n**Handling**: Updates order status to \"paid\"\n```\n\n### 4.4 configuration.md - All Configuration\n\n**Purpose**: Document every environment variable and config option.\n\n**Must include**:\n\n-   All ENV vars with types and defaults\\\n-   Required vs optional\\\n-   Examples for different environments\n\n**Example**:\n\n```markdown\n# Configuration\n\n## Environment Variables\n\n### Required\n\n- `DATABASE_URL` (string) - PostgreSQL connection string\n  - Example: `postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/dbname`\n- `KAFKA_BROKERS` (string) - Comma-separated Kafka brokers\n  - Example: `kafka-1:9092,kafka-2:9092`\n\n### Optional\n\n- `PORT` (int) - HTTP server port\n  - Default: `8080`\n- `LOG_LEVEL` (string) - Logging level\n  - Default: `info`\n  - Options: `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`\n- `MAX_CONNECTIONS` (int) - Database connection pool size\n  - Default: `10`\n\n## Configuration Files\n\n[If using config files, document structure here]\n\n## Example Configurations\n\n### Development\n```bash\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost:5432/dev\nKAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:9092\nLOG_LEVEL=debug\n```\n\n### Production\n```bash\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://prod-db:5432/orders\nKAFKA_BROKERS=kafka-1:9092,kafka-2:9092,kafka-3:9092\nLOG_LEVEL=info\nMAX_CONNECTIONS=50\n```\n```\n\n### 4.5 operations.md - Running and Deploying\n\n**Purpose**: How to operate this component in production.\n\n**Must include**:\n\n-   How to deploy\\\n-   How to monitor\\\n-   How to rollback\\\n-   Key metrics and alerts\n\n**Example**:\n\n```markdown\n# Operations Guide\n\n## Deployment\n\n### Prerequisites\n- Kubernetes cluster with access\n- Database migrations applied\n- Kafka topics created\n\n### Deploy to Dev\n```bash\nmake deploy-dev\n# or\nkubectl apply -f k8s/dev/\n```\n\n### Deploy to Production\n```bash\n# Check current version\nkubectlprod get deployment order-service\n\n# Deploy new version\nmake deploy-prod VERSION=v1.2.3\n\n# Monitor rollout\nkubectlprod rollout status deployment/order-service\n```\n\n## Monitoring\n\n### Key Metrics\n- `orders_created_total` - Total orders created\n- `orders_failed_total` - Failed order creations\n- `order_processing_duration_seconds` - Processing time\n\n### Alerts\n- **High Error Rate**: >5% errors in 5 minutes\n- **High Latency**: p99 >1s for 5 minutes\n- **Low Throughput**: <10 orders/minute\n\n### Dashboards\n- [Grafana: Order Service Overview](https://grafana.example.com/d/orders)\n\n## Rollback\n\n```bash\n# Rollback to previous version\nkubectlprod rollout undo deployment/order-service\n\n# Rollback to specific version\nkubectlprod rollout undo deployment/order-service --to-revision=5\n```\n\n## Health Checks\n\n- `/healthz` - Liveness probe (always returns 200)\n- `/readyz` - Readiness probe (checks DB + Kafka connectivity)\n```\n\n### 4.6 troubleshooting.md - Common Issues\n\n**Purpose**: Known issues and how to fix them.\n\n**Must include**:\n\n-   Common errors with solutions\\\n-   Debug procedures\\\n-   FAQ\n\n**Example**:\n\n```markdown\n# Troubleshooting\n\n## Common Issues\n\n### Database Connection Timeout\n\n**Symptom**: `pq: connection timeout` errors in logs\n\n**Cause**: Database connection pool exhausted\n\n**Solution**:\n1. Check current connections: `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;`\n2. Increase `MAX_CONNECTIONS` environment variable\n3. Check for connection leaks in code\n\n### Kafka Consumer Lag\n\n**Symptom**: Consumer lag increasing, events not processed\n\n**Cause**: Processing too slow or consumer crashed\n\n**Solution**:\n1. Check consumer group lag: `kafka-consumer-groups --describe --group order-service`\n2. Scale up consumer replicas\n3. Check for errors in processing logic\n\n## Debug Procedures\n\n### Enable Debug Logging\n```bash\n# Temporarily enable debug logging\nkubectlprod set env deployment/order-service LOG_LEVEL=debug\n\n# Remember to revert after debugging\nkubectlprod set env deployment/order-service LOG_LEVEL=info\n```\n\n### Access Logs\n```bash\n# View recent logs\nkubectlprod logs deployment/order-service --tail=100\n\n# Follow logs in real-time\nkubectlprod logs deployment/order-service -f\n\n# Filter for errors\nkubectlprod logs deployment/order-service | grep ERROR\n```\n```\n\n### 4.7 development.md - Development Workflow\n\n**Purpose**: How to work on this component locally.\n\n**Must include**:\n\n-   Local setup steps\\\n-   How to run tests\\\n-   Development workflow\\\n-   Code organization\n\n**Example**:\n\n```markdown\n# Development Guide\n\n## Local Setup\n\n### Prerequisites\n- Go 1.24+\n- Docker and Docker Compose\n- Make\n\n### Setup\n```bash\n# Clone and install dependencies\ngit clone [repo]\ncd order-service\ngo mod download\n\n# Start dependencies (DB, Kafka)\ndocker-compose up -d\n\n# Run migrations\nmake migrate\n\n# Run service\nmake run\n```\n\n## Running Tests\n\n```bash\n# Run all tests\nmake test\n\n# Run with coverage\nmake test-coverage\n\n# Run integration tests\nmake test-integration\n```\n\n## Code Organization\n\n```\norder-service/\n├── cmd/            - Main application entry\n├── internal/       - Private application code\n│   ├── handler/   - HTTP handlers\n│   ├── service/   - Business logic\n│   ├── repository/- Data access\n│   └── model/     - Domain models\n├── pkg/           - Public libraries\n└── docs/          - Documentation\n```\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n1. Create feature branch from `master`\n2. Make changes and write tests\n3. Run `make precommit` (format, test, lint)\n4. Commit and push\n5. Create pull request\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Product Requirement Definitions (PRDs)\n\n**Purpose**: Document features BEFORE implementation to provide context for AI assistants.\n\n**Full documentation**: See [prd-guide.md](prd-guide.md) for complete PRD templates, examples, and best practices.\n\n**Key points**:\n- **Stored in**: `docs/prd/`\n- **Naming**: `YYYY-NNN-feature-name.md` (e.g., `2024-001-order-cancellation.md`)\n- **When to create**: Feature requires >1 day of work, multiple approaches possible, affects multiple components\n- **Status tracking**: Draft → In Progress → Implemented → Deprecated\n\n**PRD Workflow**: For AI-assisted PRD creation and implementation workflow, see project-local PRD workflow guide.\n\nFor complete template with all sections, examples, and detailed guidance, see [prd-guide.md](prd-guide.md).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)\n\n**Purpose**: Document important architectural decisions and their rationale for future reference.\n\n**Full documentation**: See [adr-guide.md](adr-guide.md) for complete ADR templates, examples, and best practices.\n\n**Key points**:\n- **Stored in**: `docs/adr/` (root-level) or `service-name/docs/adr/` (service-specific)\n- **Naming**: `NNNN-decision-title.md` (e.g., `0001-use-kafka-for-events.md`)\n- **When to create**: Technology choices, architectural patterns, decisions affecting multiple components, expensive-to-reverse decisions\n- **Status tracking**: Proposed → Accepted → Deprecated/Superseded\n\n**Organization**:\n- **Root-level ADRs** (`docs/adr/`): Project-wide decisions affecting multiple services\n- **Service-level ADRs** (`service-name/docs/adr/`): Service-specific architectural decisions\n\nFor complete template, examples, organization guidance, and best practices, see [adr-guide.md](adr-guide.md).\n\n---\n\n## 7. When to Add Documentation\n\nAdd docs when: - A change affects users or APIs\\\n- Multiple people work on the feature\\\n- It's larger than 1--2 tickets\\\n- A decision has long-term impact\\\n- A problem might repeat\n\n---\n\n## 8. Writing for AI Context Resumption\n\n**Critical Principle**: Write documentation so an AI assistant (Claude\nCode, etc.) can understand the project state after months of absence.\n\n### What AI Assistants Need\n\n**Business Context**:\n\n-   WHY does this exist?\\\n-   What problem are we solving?\\\n-   Who are the users?\\\n-   What's the business value?\n\n**Technical Context**:\n\n-   HOW does it work?\\\n-   What patterns are used?\\\n-   What technologies are involved?\\\n-   How do components interact?\n\n**Current State**:\n\n-   What's implemented vs planned?\\\n-   What decisions were made?\\\n-   What's working vs broken?\\\n-   What are known limitations?\n\n**Operational Context**:\n\n-   How to run locally?\\\n-   How to deploy?\\\n-   How to debug?\\\n-   What's being monitored?\n\n### Writing Best Practices\n\n**Assume Zero Prior Knowledge**:\n\n```markdown\n❌ Bad: \"Use the standard deployment process\"\n✓ Good: \"Deploy using `make deploy-prod`, which builds the Docker image,\npushes to GCR, and applies Kubernetes manifests\"\n\n❌ Bad: \"Configure the usual environment variables\"\n✓ Good: \"Required environment variables: DATABASE_URL (PostgreSQL\nconnection), KAFKA_BROKERS (comma-separated list)\"\n```\n\n**Explain WHY, Not Just WHAT**:\n\n```markdown\n❌ Bad: \"We use PostgreSQL for storage\"\n✓ Good: \"We use PostgreSQL for storage because we need ACID transactions\nfor order processing and strong consistency guarantees\"\n```\n\n**Include Diagrams for System Understanding**:\n\n```markdown\n✓ Good:\n```text\nUser Request → API Gateway → Order Service → PostgreSQL\n                                   ↓\n                              Kafka (orders.created)\n                                   ↓\n                            Notification Service\n```\n```\n\n**Document Current State**:\n\n```markdown\n✓ Good:\n## Current State (2024-11-27)\n\n**Implemented**:\n- Order creation and retrieval\n- Payment processing integration\n- Email notifications\n\n**In Progress**:\n- Order cancellation (see PRD-2024-003)\n- Refund processing\n\n**Known Issues**:\n- High latency on order search (>2s) - investigating database indexes\n- Occasional Kafka consumer lag during peak hours\n\n**Planned**:\n- Partial cancellations (Q1 2025)\n- Subscription orders (Q2 2025)\n```\n\n**Keep Documentation Updated**:\n\n```markdown\n✓ Good: Add update log to long-lived docs\n\n## Updates Log\n- 2024-11-27: Added cancellation endpoint documentation\n- 2024-10-15: Updated deployment process for new Kubernetes cluster\n- 2024-09-01: Initial version\n```\n\n**Link Related Documentation**:\n\n```markdown\n✓ Good:\nThis order cancellation feature (PRD-2024-003) requires:\n- [Payment refund integration](./integration/payment-service.md)\n- [Kafka event schema update](../adr/0015-order-event-schema.md)\n- [Database migration](./operations.md#migrations)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Style Rules\n\n### Do:\n\n-   Keep files focused\\\n-   Use examples\\\n-   Link between docs (use relative links)\\\n-   Use diagrams when helpful\\\n-   Keep docs up-to-date (remove outdated content)\\\n-   Version diagrams with dates or git commits\\\n-   **Write for AI assistants**: Assume reader has no prior knowledge\\\n-   **Explain WHY**: Not just what was done, but why it was done that\n    way\\\n-   **Document current state**: What works, what's broken, what's\n    planned\n\n### Don't:\n\n-   Duplicate content\\\n-   Write unnecessarily long text\\\n-   Document obvious code patterns (let GoDoc handle that)\\\n-   Create extensive docs for every tiny component\\\n-   **Assume prior knowledge**: AI has no memory of previous work\\\n-   **Skip business context**: Technical details without business\n    reasoning\\\n-   **Leave docs stale**: Outdated docs are worse than no docs\n\n---\n\n## 10. Multi-Service Projects / Monorepos\n\nFor projects containing multiple services (like `ecommerce-platform`, `inventory-system`):\n\n### Repository Root Structure\n\n    README.md           → High-level overview of entire project\n    docs/               → Project-wide documentation\n    ├── architecture.md → Overall system architecture\n    ├── development.md  → Development workflows, tooling\n    ├── deployment.md   → Project-wide deployment procedures\n    ├── adr/           → Cross-service architectural decisions\n    └── prd/           → Project-wide features\n    <service-a>/\n    ├── README.md      → Service-specific quickstart\n    ├── docs/          → Service-specific docs (optional)\n    <service-b>/\n    ├── README.md      → Service-specific quickstart\n    └── docs/          → Service-specific docs (optional)\n\n### Root README.md\n\nThe root README provides project-wide orientation.\n\nMulti-service projects are **always internal**, so keep it minimal\n(50-100 lines).\n\n**Required**:\n\n-   Brief project purpose\\\n-   List of key services (or link to service list)\\\n-   Essential daily commands\\\n-   Link to docs/\n\n**Template**:\n\n```markdown\n# [Project Name]\n\n> Brief one-line description\n\n[Optional: One paragraph context if needed]\n\n## Services\n\nSee [docs/services.md](./docs/services.md) for full service list.\n\nKey services:\n- [service-name](./service-name/) - Brief description\n- [service-name](./service-name/) - Brief description\n- [service-name](./service-name/) - Brief description\n\n## Quick Commands\n\n```bash\n# Run all tests\nmake test\n\n# Update all dependencies\nmake gomodupdate\n\n# Precommit checks\nmake precommit\n\n# Deploy to dev\nmake deploy-dev\n\n# Deploy to prod\nmake deploy-prod\n```\n\n## Documentation\n\nSee [docs/](./docs/) for detailed documentation:\n\n- [Architecture](./docs/architecture.md)\n- [Development Guide](./docs/development.md)\n- [Deployment](./docs/deployment.md)\n```\n\n### Root docs/\n\nProject-wide documentation that affects multiple services.\n\n-   Cross-service architecture diagrams\\\n-   Shared infrastructure (Kafka, databases, monitoring)\\\n-   Development workflows (commit process, testing, CI/CD)\\\n-   Deployment procedures affecting multiple services\\\n-   Common patterns used across services\n\n### Service-Level README.md\n\n**Each service MUST have its own README.md** following the standard structure (Section 2).\n\n-   Overview of the specific service\\\n-   How to run this service locally\\\n-   Service-specific configuration\\\n-   Link to root docs/ for shared infrastructure\n\n### Service-Level docs/\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Most services should have their own `docs/` folder for\nAI context.\n\n**Purpose**: Provide comprehensive context about THIS specific service\nfor AI assistants.\n\n**When to create** (generally always for non-trivial services):\n\n-   Service has unique business logic\\\n-   Service has its own API or interfaces\\\n-   Service has specific operational concerns\\\n-   **AI needs context to work on this service**\n\n**Recommended structure** (use Section 4 templates):\n\n``` markdown\nservice-name/\n├── README.md              → Minimal quickstart (50-100 lines)\n└── docs/\n    ├── overview.md        → Business + technical overview\n    ├── architecture.md    → How this service works\n    ├── api.md             → Service-specific API/interfaces\n    ├── configuration.md   → Environment variables\n    ├── operations.md      → Deploy, monitor, troubleshoot\n    ├── prd/              → Service-specific features\n    └── adr/              → Service-specific decisions\n```\n\n**Key principle**: README stays minimal, `docs/` contains all the\ncontext AI needs.\n\n### When to Document at Root vs Service Level\n\n  ---\n  Root docs/                    Service docs/\n  ----------------------------- ------------------------------------------\n  Cross-service data flow       Service-specific API endpoints\n\n  Shared Kafka topics           Service-unique business logic\n\n  Common deployment process     Service-specific troubleshooting\n\n  Shared monitoring setup       Service-unique configuration\n\n  Project-wide ADRs             Service-specific decisions\n  ---\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n  ---\n  File / Folder             Purpose\n  ------------------------- ----------------------------------------------\n  README.md                 Quickstart + high-level overview\n\n  docs/                     Detailed technical documentation\n\n  docs/prd/                 Product Requirement Definitions\n\n  docs/adr/                 Long-term architectural decisions\n\n  docs/api.md               Full interface and endpoint documentation\n\n  docs/architecture.md      Internal design and system structure\n\n  docs/configuration.md     Full configuration reference\n\n  docs/operations.md        Deployment and operations\n\n  docs/troubleshooting.md   Known issues and fixes\n  ---\n","numLines":1159,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1159}}}
